              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A storage `extend_ttl` or `set_ttl` call uses an integer literal outside the documented expected range of TTL ledgers. A zero or very small TTL expires entries almost immediately, while a value above the network maximum is rejected or signals a unit mistake (for example seconds instead of ledgers). Either case can cause lost data or unexpected transaction failures. The detector only inspects literal constants, not computed expressions."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## TTL constant outside the expected range\n\nA storage `extend_ttl` or `set_ttl` call uses an integer literal outside the documented expected range of TTL ledgers. A zero or very small TTL expires entries almost immediately, while a value above the network maximum is rejected or signals a unit mistake (for example seconds instead of ledgers). Either case can cause lost data or unexpected transaction failures. The detector only inspects literal constants, not computed expressions.\n\n### Remediation\n\nDefine named TTL constants based on the contract's retention policy and the Soroban network maximum (about six months of ledgers), then pass those constants to `extend_ttl`/`set_ttl`. Use the same policy for every key so entries expire predictably instead of using ad-hoc literals.\n\n### References\n\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/state-archival",
                "text": "A storage `extend_ttl` or `set_ttl` call uses an integer literal outside the documented expected range of TTL ledgers. A zero or very small TTL expires entries almost immediately, while a value above the network maximum is rejected or signals a unit mistake (for example seconds instead of ledgers). Either case can cause lost data or unexpected transaction failures. The detector only inspects literal constants, not computed expressions.\n\nRemediation: Define named TTL constants based on the contract's retention policy and the Soroban network maximum (about six months of ledgers), then pass those constants to `extend_ttl`/`set_ttl`. Use the same policy for every key so entries expire predictably instead of using ad-hoc literals."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
              "id": "SS-026",
              "name": "TTL constant outside the expected range",
              "properties": {
                "category": "resource-usage",
                "confidence": "low",
                "references": [
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/persisting-data",
                  "https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/storage/state-archival"
                ],
                "remediation": "Define named TTL constants based on the contract's retention policy and the Soroban network maximum (about six months of ledgers), then pass those constants to `extend_ttl`/`set_ttl`. Use the same policy for every key so entries expire predictably instead of using ad-hoc literals.",
                "severity": "low"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TTL constant outside the expected range"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Passing `env.current_contract_address()` to `require_auth` authorizes the contract itself instead of the caller. This is almost always a logic error: the check succeeds for anyone who can invoke the contract, so it does not provide the intended access control."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "## Contract's own address used as an authorization subject\n\nPassing `env.current_contract_address()` to `require_auth` authorizes the contract itself instead of the caller. This is almost always a logic error: the check succeeds for anyone who can invoke the contract, so it does not provide the intended access control.\n\n### Remediation\n\nAuthorize the actual caller instead: take an `Address` parameter and call `caller.require_auth()`, or call `require_auth` on the stored owner/admin address. Reserve `current_contract_address()` for checks that really concern this contract's identity.\n\n### References\n\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security/authorization\n- https://developers.stellar.org/docs/learn/encyclopedia/security",
                "text": "Passing `env.current_contract_address()` to `require_auth` authorizes the contract itself instead of the caller. This is almost always a logic error: the check succeeds for anyone who can invoke the contract, so it does not provide the intended access control.\n\nRemediation: Authorize the actual caller instead: take an `Address` parameter and call `caller.require_auth()`, or call `require_auth` on the stored owner/admin address. Reserve `current_contract_address()` for checks that really concern this contract's identity."
              },
